{
  "markdown_report": "**Insights from the starting point.** (1) Training margins measure sampled-pair separation, with scale and sampling dependence; they cannot replace validation HR@10. (2) Feedback motivates testing recent, repeated evidence, but establishes neither unmet need nor compatibility. Recency pooling emphasizes repetition without explicitly identifying recurring interests. (3) The compatibility interface supports architectural changes only when training, prediction, and diagnostic features remain consistent; missing observations must remain distinguishable from healthy results.\n\n**Insights from related work.** (1) [Krichene and Rendle](https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/) establish sampled-ranking inconsistency; retain the pinned objective. (2) [Fossil](https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.09152) and [HGN](https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.09217) substantially precede mixed temporal representations and gated pooling, reducing originality. (3) [Hooker et al.](https://research.google/pubs/evaluating-feature-importance-estimates/) caution that deletion creates distribution shift; [Liu et al.](https://doi.org/10.1145/3705328.3748042) identify availability confounding. Sensitivity demonstrates model dependence, not relevance.\n\n**Directions and framework.** Organize research around objective alignment, temporal evidence, and metadata support:\n\n| Direction | Existing signal | Gap | Proposed extension |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Objective alignment | Training margins | Sampling, scale, selection bias | Pinned validation; paired bootstrap; attempt ledger |\n| Temporal evidence | SASRec attention | Unmeasured contribution of explicit recency | Gated pooling with matched controls |\n| Metadata support | Titles/descriptions | Uncertain functional relationships | Evidence spans with abstention |\n\n**Candidate algorithms.** Scores are originality/potential/difficulty; higher difficulty means harder implementation.\n\n1. **Matched history-ablation adapter — 3/6/4.** Compare targeted and repeated matched random removals [Hooker; Liu]. Originality: useful integration, but established methods. Potential: reveals dependence, but cannot establish relevance. Difficulty: reuses inference, but position controls are delicate.\n2. **Gated recency residual — 3/6/4; selected.** Add normalized exponential history pooling to SASRec [Fossil; HGN]. Originality: a specific integration, but substantial precedents. Potential: tests a concrete hypothesis, but SASRec already learns temporal relevance. Difficulty: compact architecture, but coordinated serialization and ablations remain necessary.\n3. **Evidence-supported metadata reranking — 5/6/6.** Bound title/description similarity contributions and abstain on missing evidence. Originality: evidence-aware combination, but familiar lexical scoring. Potential: inspectable relevance signals, but similarity cannot establish compatibility. Difficulty: available inputs suffice, but extraction and calibration expand scope.\n\n**Selected implementation.** At 0% progress, prioritize the small residual. Replace the original convex mixture with identity-preserving `z=h+tanh(a)*P(r)`, initializing `a=0` and `P` nonzero. This preserves baseline features initially; projection gradients begin after the gate moves. Compute causal, padding-aware FP32 pooling:\n\n`S=0; Z=0; for valid x: S=λ*S+x; Z=λ*Z+1; r=S/Z`\n\nPadding leaves states unchanged and outputs masked. Include the observed item, exclude its next-item target, and normalize independently per position. Use event-index decay initially; timestamp decay requires separately aligned units.\n\nPredeclare uniform, last-item, and exponential controls with identical fusion; retain baseline loss, sampling, history window, and training allowance. Track branch norm, alignment, score changes, and disabled-branch validation. Preserve four routes, seeds, environment DATA_DIR, predictor writer, and diagnostic entry points. Select only by pinned macro validation HR@10. Log every attempt; paired bootstrap intervals condition on fixed candidates and do not correct selection optimism [Cawley–Talbot](https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v11/cawley10a.html).",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Add an identity-initialized gated residual containing a causal, normalized exponentially weighted history embedding to each category's SASRec model. Compare its contribution with uniform pooling, last-item pooling, and controlled history perturbations.",
    "motivation": "Qualitative feedback suggests insufficient emphasis on recent, repeated interests. Explicit temporal pooling provides a small intervention whose incremental contribution can be measured against SASRec's existing attention. Repeated occurrences receive repeated weight, but the branch does not explicitly recognize recurring themes, compatibility, ownership, satisfaction, or unmet need.",
    "implementation_notes": "Preserve preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, all four category routes, DATA_DIR=os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], trial-local artifacts, and the SASREC_EPOCHS environment default of 200. Keep baseline loss, item scoring, negative sampling, history window, optimizer, and checkpoint-evaluation schedule fixed initially. Use h + tanh(a)*P(r), with a separate scalar a per category initialized to zero and a nonzero bias-free projection P initialized using the baseline initialization convention. This replaces the earlier convex mixture and preserves baseline feature values at initialization; the zero gate initially blocks projection gradients, while its own gradient can be nonzero. Shared item embeddings still receive baseline gradients. Accumulate the pooling numerator and denominator in FP32 using S=lambda*S+x and Z=lambda*Z+1 at each valid event; padding changes neither state, and padded outputs are zero. Normalize only within each causal prefix. The current observed item is allowed, its target is excluded, and inference extracts the same last valid feature used by baseline prediction. Predeclare lambda in {0, 0.5, 0.9, 1}: zero is last-item pooling, one is uniform pooling, and the intermediate values test exponential decay. Use one shared lambda configuration across categories, with category-specific learned gates. Train the unchanged baseline and these four configurations sequentially on one GPU within the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget, recording elapsed GPU time and every failed attempt; do not assume all configurations fit. Evaluate at the baseline's unchanged checkpoint schedule, select the highest equal-weight four-category validation HR@10 among completed eligible checkpoints, and resolve ties by earlier checkpoint then predeclared configuration order. Freeze the pipeline after selection. Serialize architecture version, lambda, projection, gate, and model configuration beneath the trial directory; update the predictor writer and compatibility view together. view.model remains the actual nn.Module, view.log2feats returns fused features, and view.predict_score uses the trained scoring function. DiagnosisProbe(view, device) must call view.model.eval(), run under no_grad, restore the prior training state, and retain run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), returning metrics and metric_definitions. diagnosis_interpreter_prompt(raw_diagnosis) must incorporate both. Preserve existing embedding and margin probes, add valid counts, and represent empty samples as unavailable rather than healthy zero-valued measurements. Category lists remain empty because item categories are unavailable. Measure residual-to-encoder norm ratio, cosine alignment where defined, score changes, and branch-disabled validation; gate size alone does not establish contribution. For descriptive perturbation checks, use fixed candidates and deterministic tie handling. Compare newest-prefix removals against repeated random removals matched on count and coarse recency strata; report residual differences in exact event age. Preserve surviving-item order. Analyze compaction separately from position-preserving masking, documenting positional indices, padding, attention masks, and final-state extraction; masking can itself be out of distribution. Record deletion curves and targeted-minus-random changes without interpreting them as causal relevance. Optional follow-ups, only if budget remains, include fixed versus learned mixing, a parameter-matched extra encoder projection, older-history shuffling with the last item fixed, and removing the newest event from the pooled branch. Timestamp decay is deferred because it requires aligned timestamps and consistent elapsed-time units. Bootstrap paired users within each category and average category differences equally; intervals condition on trained models and fixed candidates and exclude training variability, candidate-sampling uncertainty, and selection optimism. The twenty feedback users provide exploratory full-catalog behavior checks only. Use titles and descriptions solely where observed; never infer unavailable ratings, reviews, categories, prices, stores, simulator traits, ownership, or compatibility. Reserve generalization claims for the host's independent held-out evaluation.",
    "pseudocode": "Predeclare configuration order: unchanged SASRec, lambda=0, lambda=0.5, lambda=0.9, lambda=1.\nFor each configuration, subject to the remaining shared GPU budget:\n  Reset prescribed training seed and retain baseline training/evaluation schedule.\n  For each of the four dataset routes:\n    Initialize SASRec; for residual configurations initialize nonzero P and a=0.\n    For each training batch:\n      h = baseline SASRec sequence features.\n      S = zeros(batch, dimension, FP32); Z = zeros(batch, 1, FP32).\n      For each sequence position t:\n        valid = sequence[:, t] != 0.\n        x = item_emb(sequence[:, t]).float().\n        Update S=lambda*S+x and Z=lambda*Z+1 only where valid.\n        r[:, t] = S / clamp_min(Z, epsilon) where valid, otherwise zero.\n      z = h for baseline; otherwise h + tanh(a)*P(r).\n      Mask padded features and optimize unchanged baseline next-item loss.\n    Save trial-local checkpoint and matching predictor configuration.\n  Obtain pinned validation results with unchanged users, positives, and 99 unique negatives.\n  Record all four category HR@10 values and their equal-weight mean.\nSelect the eligible checkpoint with highest macro validation HR@10 using the predeclared tie rule.\nFreeze its predictor and run measured compatibility diagnostics.\nReport paired uncertainty and controlled perturbation results as exploratory diagnostics, separately from the selection objective.",
    "originality": {
      "score": 3,
      "positive": "The precise SASRec residual, identity initialization, and controlled diagnostic integration form a concrete, testable combination.",
      "negative": "Fossil, HGN, temporal pooling, and gated residual methods provide substantial prior art; the proposal is an incremental architectural study."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 6,
      "positive": "Endpoint controls can reveal when explicit recency contributes beyond learned attention and support later studies of temporal inductive bias across domains.",
      "negative": "The branch may duplicate attention, exploit recent-item similarity, or suppress durable interests; sensitivity and benchmark gains alone do not establish improved user relevance."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 4,
      "positive": "The core model requires a short causal recurrence, a projection, and one gate per category.",
      "negative": "Reliable execution requires synchronized predictor serialization, causal masking, diagnostic adaptation, budget accounting, and carefully specified comparison controls."
    }
  },
  "related_work": [
    {
      "title": "On Sampled Metrics for Item Recommendation",
      "link": "https://research.google/pubs/on-sampled-metrics-for-item-recommendation/",
      "contributions": [
        "Establishes that sampled recommendation metrics can disagree with full-catalog model rankings."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not establish that auxiliary margins, hard-negative rankings, or full-catalog behavior predict improvement under the pinned sampled benchmark."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Scarcelli et al. (2025): sequence-edit recommendation explanations",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.03606",
      "contributions": [
        "Studies changes in recommendations through searches over sequence edits, according to the supplied research summary."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Model sensitivity and explanation fidelity do not establish causal preferences or recommendation relevance."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Liu et al. (RecSys 2025): availability effects in history shuffling",
      "link": "https://doi.org/10.1145/3705328.3748042",
      "contributions": [
        "Identifies item-availability confounding in arbitrary history shuffling, according to the supplied research summary."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Availability controls do not eliminate distribution shift or all ambiguities in perturbation interpretation."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Fusing Similarity Models with Markov Chains for Sparse Sequential Recommendation",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.09152",
      "contributions": [
        "Combines similarity-based long-term preferences with short-term Markov dynamics, providing close structural precedent for mixed temporal signals."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not establish the effectiveness of an exponentially pooled SASRec residual under this benchmark."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Hierarchical Gating Networks for Sequential Recommendation",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1906.09217",
      "contributions": [
        "Uses feature and instance gates over recent item embeddings and combines aggregated history with other preference signals."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Its gating and prediction structure differs from a scalar-gated exponential residual; benchmark gains cannot be transferred to this setting."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Self-Attentive Sequential Recommendation",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.09781",
      "contributions": [
        "Introduces SASRec's attention-based selection of relevant historical interactions."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "The proposed residual must demonstrate incremental value beyond temporal patterns already learned by attention."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Evaluating Feature Importance Estimates",
      "link": "https://research.google/pubs/evaluating-feature-importance-estimates/",
      "contributions": [
        "Highlights distribution-shift problems in feature-removal evaluation."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not directly validate a particular sequential-history intervention or convert dependence into evidence of relevance."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "ReZero is All You Need: Fast Convergence at Large Depth",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2003.04887",
      "contributions": [
        "Supports zero-initialized residual gates as a means of starting from an identity mapping."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not establish recommendation accuracy gains; a zero gate initially blocks gradients to branch-specific parameters."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "On Over-fitting in Model Selection and Subsequent Selection Bias in Performance Evaluation",
      "link": "https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v11/cawley10a.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Explains how repeated model selection can overfit evaluation criteria and bias reported performance."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Ordinary bootstrap intervals for the selected configuration do not remove selection optimism."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "pandas exponentially weighted window documentation",
      "link": "https://pandas.pydata.org/pandas-docs/stable/reference/api/pandas.DataFrame.ewm.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Defines normalized exponentially weighted averaging and distinguishes decay parameterizations."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Provides numerical definitions rather than evidence of recommendation effectiveness; padding-aware causal implementation remains the model author's responsibility."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
